d2d1: Keep our device context state bound across draws.

Each primitive Direct2D drew swapped its own ID3DDeviceContextState in and
the application's back out: two full state switches per draw, each a
state comparison and a burst of commands on wined3d's queue. With hundreds
of primitives per frame that was a large part of Affinity's cost of
drawing selection outlines, handles and node edits.

Keep our state bound from the first draw and give the application its
state back where it can use the device context again: at EndDraw, Flush
and SetTarget, when the context is destroyed, and before another device
context draws on the same Direct3D device, so that ours is never saved as
the application's. Native Direct2D only submits its batched drawing at
those points too. WINE_D2D_NO_STATE_BATCHING=1 restores the old
behaviour.

The d2d1 conformance tests give the same result before and after (17491
tests, 0 failures). In Affinity under a nested KWin, three rounds each,
CPU per presented frame on wined3d's command thread and the UI thread:

                 before          after
  drag         7.5 / 11.4 ms   6.0 / 10.3 ms   (frames 216 -> 234)
  node edit    7.3 / 10.3 ms   5.7 /  9.0 ms   (frames 164 -> 179)
  zoom         9.4 / 12.2 ms   7.7 / 10.3 ms

Selections, handles and node editing drew correctly.

